Vācaspati Miśra
(9th century A. D.)
------------------------------------------
One of the greatest scholars and writers in the post-शङ्कर period, वाचस्पति मिश्र lived in मिथिला when the king ruling there was नृग. He is assigned to the 9th century A. D.
His most celebrated work is the Bhāmatī, his gloss on the bhāṣya of शङ्कर (A. D. 788-820) on the Brahma-sūtras. This has six sub-commentaries. His interpretation of Advaita Vedānta has given rise to the भामती School and the avacchedavāda.
He was a prolific writer on almost all the facets of Hindu philosophy.
The following is the list of his works:
Nyāyakaṇikā on Maṇḍana’s (9th century A. D.) Vidhiviveka;
Brahmatattvasamīkṣā on Maṇḍana’s Brahmasiddhi;
Tattvabindu (a discussion of language in its relation to meaning);
Nyāyavārttikatātparyaṭīkā (a commentary on Udyotakara’s [A. D. 635] Nyāyavārttika);
Nyāyasūcīnibandha (a supplement to the afore-mentioned work);
Sāṅkhyatattvakaumudī on the Sāṅkhyakārikā of ईश्वरकृष्ण (A. D. 350);
Tattvavaiśāradī, gloss on the Vyāsabhāṣya, on the Yogasūtras of पतञ्जलि (2nd century B. C.).
All these works have been published except the second one.
See also BHĀMATĪ.
